From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 01:44:04 +0200
Subject: phy: intel: grx500-pcie: pass the chip-top offset as a syscon
 argument

The USB PHY on this SoC reaches its chip-top register as

	intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x34>;

while this driver names the same syscon with no argument at all and
keeps the offset to itself as PCIE_IFMUX_CFG. Two properties on one node
addressing one block in two different ways is the first thing a reader
asks about, and only one of the two says where in the syscon the driver
is going to write.

The PCIe side was left alone when the USB half was converted, on the
grounds that it carries two shift values that are not offsets into the
syscon and would not collapse the same way. That is true, but it
describes the root complex driver rather than this one: the shifts are
intel,inbound-shift and intel,outbound-shift and they belong to
pcie-intel-gw.c. This PHY has no shift values. Its entire use of the
chip-top is one fixed register with one bit position derived from
intel,phy-id, which is the shape the helper exists for.

The other reason to wait was that the change wants a board with working
PCIe to retest on, and only one of the two boards here has any. The
second one gains all three root complexes in the changes that follow
this one, so both will exercise the new form.

Mixed arity on one provider is fine and is already the situation:
syscon_regmap_lookup_by_phandle_args() parses with
of_parse_phandle_with_fixed_args(), so the count is the caller's and
&chiptop needs no #syscon-cells. The three root complexes go on naming
it with no argument, untouched.

Conservative form only. intel,phy-id stays as its own property. The
offset is the same 0x120 for all three instances and the id selects a
bit inside that one word, so whether the id should ride along as a
second argument is a different question with a different answer, and it
is not settled here.

The offset has three consumers rather than one, so all three move
together: the register dump, the reference-clock gate, and the lookup.
Converting only the lookup compiles, boots and behaves exactly as
before while silently ignoring the argument the DT now supplies, which
is the way this change is easiest to get wrong.

Doing it now is close to free. The property has never been documented,
nothing outside this tree reads it, and there is no binding to keep in
step, so there is no compatibility question to answer -- which stops
being true the moment the zero-argument form is published.

The DT half has to move in the same commit: the blob is built into the
kernel image on this platform, so a driver that reads one form and a
dtsi that spells the other never coexist in a bootable image. Board
files are unaffected; the property is set once per instance at SoC
level.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
index 6cda1e5d7b0d..e32f4a73f4c3 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
@@ -239,7 +239,7 @@ pcie_phy0: phy@18500000 {
 		reg = <0x18500000 0x100000>;
 		reg-names = "phy";
 		#phy-cells = <0>;
-		intel,syscon = <&chiptop>;
+		intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x120>;
 		intel,cgu-syscon = <&cgu>;
 		intel,phy-id = <0>;
 		resets = <&rcu_reset0 12 12>;
@@ -252,7 +252,7 @@ pcie_phy1: phy@18000000 {
 		reg = <0x18000000 0x100000>;
 		reg-names = "phy";
 		#phy-cells = <0>;
-		intel,syscon = <&chiptop>;
+		intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x120>;
 		intel,cgu-syscon = <&cgu>;
 		intel,phy-id = <1>;
 		resets = <&rcu_reset0 13 13>;
@@ -265,7 +265,7 @@ pcie_phy2: phy@18a00000 {
 		reg = <0x18a00000 0x100000>;
 		reg-names = "phy";
 		#phy-cells = <0>;
-		intel,syscon = <&chiptop>;
+		intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x120>;
 		intel,cgu-syscon = <&cgu>;
 		intel,phy-id = <2>;
 		resets = <&rcu_reset0 10 10>;
diff --git a/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-pcie.c b/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-pcie.c
index c0f0a4a5fe31..79ccdedd083f 100644
--- a/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-pcie.c
@@ -47,8 +47,13 @@
 #define PCIE_LCPLL_LOCK_SLEEP_US	100
 #define PCIE_LCPLL_LOCK_TIMEOUT_US	(100 * USEC_PER_MSEC)
 
-/* CHIPTOP interface mux */
-#define PCIE_IFMUX_CFG			0x120
+/*
+ * This instance's reference-clock gate bit in the chip-top interface mux
+ * word, reached through intel,syscon at the byte offset that property's
+ * argument gives (0x120). Unlike the USB PHY's per-controller word the
+ * offset is the same for all three instances, and which instance is meant
+ * is a bit position inside it, which is what intel,phy-id supplies.
+ */
 #define PCIE_RCLK(id)			BIT((id) + 22)
 
 #define PCIE_PHY_ID_MAX			2
@@ -67,6 +72,7 @@ struct intel_pciephy {
 	void __iomem *phy_base;
 	struct regmap *cgu;
 	struct regmap *chiptop;
+	unsigned int ifmux_off;
 	struct phy *phy;
 	struct reset_control *rst;
 	u32 id;
@@ -94,7 +100,7 @@ static void intel_pciephy_dbg_dump(struct intel_pciephy *priv)
 		dev_dbg(dev, "LCPLL COEF[%d]: 0x%08x\n", i, val);
 	}
 
-	regmap_read(priv->chiptop, PCIE_IFMUX_CFG, &val);
+	regmap_read(priv->chiptop, priv->ifmux_off, &val);
 	dev_dbg(dev, "PCIE PHY IFMUX[22-24]: 0x%08x\n", val);
 }
 
@@ -212,7 +218,7 @@ static void intel_pciephy_reset(struct intel_pciephy *priv)
 static void intel_pciephy_ref_clk(struct intel_pciephy *priv, bool on)
 {
 	/* 0 is enable, 1 is disable */
-	regmap_update_bits(priv->chiptop, PCIE_IFMUX_CFG, PCIE_RCLK(priv->id),
+	regmap_update_bits(priv->chiptop, priv->ifmux_off, PCIE_RCLK(priv->id),
 			   on ? 0 : PCIE_RCLK(priv->id));
 }
 
@@ -282,7 +288,9 @@ static int intel_pciephy_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(priv->cgu),
 				     "failed to get the CGU syscon\n");
 
-	priv->chiptop = syscon_regmap_lookup_by_phandle(np, "intel,syscon");
+	priv->chiptop = syscon_regmap_lookup_by_phandle_args(np, "intel,syscon",
+							     1,
+							     &priv->ifmux_off);
 	if (IS_ERR(priv->chiptop))
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(priv->chiptop),
 				     "failed to get the CHIPTOP syscon\n");
